        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          These two products share a category name and almost nothing else. Asphalt sealer is a black surface coating that replaces binder the sun has destroyed. Concrete sealer is usually a clear liquid that soaks in and blocks water. Swapping them does not give you a compromise — it gives you a failure.
        </p>

        <div id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Core Differences at a Glance
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Asphalt Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete Sealer</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How it works</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coats the surface with a film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually penetrates and blocks pores</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opaque black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually clear; some add sheen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main enemy it fights</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV light and oxidation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water, freeze-thaw and de-icing salt</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical chemistry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Asphalt or coal tar emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silane, siloxane, acrylic or epoxy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface it protects</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible petroleum-bound aggregate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rigid cement-bound aggregate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years (emulsion)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-10 years depending on type</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Restores appearance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, dramatically</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minimally; may deepen colour</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Squeegee or spray, two coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sprayer or roller, one to two coats</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The single most useful distinction: asphalt sealer is a sacrificial layer you put on top, and concrete sealer is usually a treatment you put into the surface. That difference explains almost everything else, including why one turns your driveway black and the other leaves it looking much as it did.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Asphalt Sealer Is Actually Doing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Asphalt is aggregate held together by bitumen, a petroleum binder. That binder is what makes the surface black, flexible and waterproof, and ultraviolet light steadily destroys it. As it oxidises, the surface fades from black to grey, becomes brittle, and eventually releases its aggregate — the loose stones underfoot on an old driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealer addresses this by laying a fresh, opaque, sacrificial film across the top. That film absorbs the UV instead of the binder underneath, sheds water before it can reach the base, and resists the fuel and oil drips that dissolve bitumen. It wears away over two to three years and gets renewed, which is the entire maintenance model.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why It Is Black and Opaque</h3>

          <p>
            The opacity is functional rather than cosmetic. A clear coating over asphalt would let UV straight through to the binder it is meant to protect. The dramatic visual restoration people associate with sealing is a side effect of the mechanism, not the purpose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Cannot Do</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It cannot fill cracks. It is a thin film with no structural capacity; cracks need dedicated filler first.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It cannot repair a failed base. Alligator cracking and sunken sections are structural problems.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It cannot restore ravelled asphalt. Once aggregate is coming loose, sealing does not glue it back.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It cannot be applied to concrete with any useful effect, for reasons covered below.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Concrete Sealer Is Actually Doing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete has the opposite problem. Its binder is portland cement, which is not damaged by UV in any comparable way — a concrete driveway does not fade and go brittle in the sun the way asphalt does. What destroys concrete is water getting into its pore structure, freezing, expanding and spalling the surface off, a process that de-icing salt accelerates dramatically.
          </p>

          <p>
            So concrete sealers are built to stop water rather than to block light, and they come in two broad families that work in genuinely different ways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating Sealers (Silane, Siloxane)</h3>

          <p>
            These soak into the concrete and react chemically with it, lining the pores with a water-repellent layer without forming a surface film. The concrete still looks like bare concrete, still breathes, and still has its original traction. Because the protection is inside the slab rather than on top of it, penetrating sealers are not worn off by traffic and commonly last five to ten years. This is the right choice for most exposed driveways in freeze-thaw and salt country.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Film-Forming Sealers (Acrylic, Epoxy, Urethane)</h3>

          <p>
            These sit on the surface as a coating, much as asphalt sealer does, and they usually add sheen and deepen the colour. They protect well against stains and give decorative concrete a wet look, but they can be slippery when wet, they wear where traffic is heaviest, and if moisture gets trapped beneath them they can cloud or peel. Typical life is three to five years.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Decision That Matters for Concrete</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Exposed driveway: penetrate. Decorative or covered: film.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For an open driveway that sees winter salt and freeze-thaw, a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer is almost always the better technical answer. Film-forming acrylics earn their place on stamped or decorative concrete where appearance is part of the point, and in covered areas where standing water and slip risk are not concerns.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Happens If You Use the Wrong Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both mistakes are common, and they fail in different ways.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Asphalt Sealer on Concrete</h3>

          <p>
            It will go on, and it will look startling — an opaque black coating over a grey slab. The problem is bonding. Asphalt emulsion is formulated to bond with the bitumen already in asphalt pavement, and concrete offers it nothing chemically compatible to grip. The result is a coating sitting mechanically on a smooth, non-porous surface, and it peels, flakes and tracks. It also traps moisture trying to escape the slab, which accelerates spalling underneath. And it is essentially irreversible without aggressive mechanical removal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Concrete Sealer on Asphalt</h3>

          <p>
            This fails more quietly. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer is designed to react with cementitious material; asphalt has none, so there is nothing for it to react with. It does not meaningfully penetrate, does not block UV, and leaves the binder oxidising exactly as before. You will have spent money and a weekend and changed nothing measurable. A film-forming acrylic will at least form a layer, but a clear one, which lets UV through to the binder it should be shielding.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The One Legitimate Overlap</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Some acrylics are rated for both surfaces.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A small number of acrylic sealers are formulated and explicitly labelled for use on asphalt and concrete alike, and they are genuinely useful on mixed driveways where an asphalt drive meets a concrete apron. Read the label rather than assuming. If a product does not name your surface, it is not for your surface.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="which-do-i-have" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Tell Which Surface You Have
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually obvious, occasionally not — particularly on an old driveway that has been sealed several times, or one that has been resurfaced.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Asphalt is black or grey-black, slightly flexible underfoot in summer heat, and shows visible aggregate stones bound in a dark matrix.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Concrete is grey or off-white, rigid, and normally has control joints cut or tooled into it in a regular grid.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Asphalt softens noticeably on a hot day; concrete does not change at all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Concrete driveways almost always have deliberate expansion and control joints. Asphalt driveways generally do not.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A previously sealed concrete slab can look black; check an edge, a broken corner or an unsealed margin for the true colour underneath.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Mixed driveways are common — an asphalt drive with a concrete apron at the garage, or a concrete pad with an asphalt approach. Seal each with the correct product for that section rather than looking for one product that covers both, unless you have specifically found an acrylic rated for both. The join will look slightly different either way, and matching the appearance is not worth using the wrong chemistry on half the area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing Between the Concrete Sealer Types
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have established that you need a concrete sealer rather than an asphalt one, there is a second decision that matters just as much, because the two families protect in genuinely different ways and suit different situations.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How It Works</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lifespan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best Use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smallest molecule; penetrates deepest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dense concrete, heavy salt exposure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Larger molecule; penetrates well</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General driveway use, porous concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Forms a surface film, adds sheen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Decorative and stamped concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard film, excellent chemical resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garage floors, not exterior driveways</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tough, UV-stable film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High-traffic interior and covered areas</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For an Open Driveway, Penetrate</h3>

          <p>
            Silane and siloxane sealers react with the concrete and line its pore structure from within, leaving no film on top. That has three practical consequences that matter outdoors: traffic cannot wear the protection away, the slab still breathes so trapped moisture is not an issue, and the surface keeps its original traction rather than becoming slick when wet. In freeze-thaw and de-icing salt country, this is the technically correct answer for an exposed driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For Decorative Concrete, Consider a Film</h3>

          <p>
            Acrylics earn their place where appearance is part of the point. Stamped, stained or exposed-aggregate concrete usually looks considerably better under a film-forming acrylic, which deepens colour and adds the wet look that decorative work is often designed around. The trade-offs are real — shorter life, wear in traffic lanes, slip risk when wet, and the possibility of clouding if moisture is trapped beneath — but for a decorative patio or a covered area they are acceptable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy and polyurethane belong indoors or under cover. Epoxy in particular is not UV-stable in most formulations and will amber and chalk in direct sunlight, which is why it is the standard choice for garage floors and the wrong choice for the driveway leading up to them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Asphalt Sealer to a Concrete Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Asphalt emulsion is formulated to bond with the bitumen in asphalt pavement, and concrete offers nothing chemically compatible. The coating peels and flakes, tracks onto shoes and tyres, traps moisture trying to leave the slab, and removing it means aggressive mechanical work on a surface that never needed coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Penetrating Concrete Sealer to Asphalt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Silane and siloxane sealers work by reacting with cementitious material. Asphalt contains none, so the product does not meaningfully penetrate or protect, and it does nothing at all about the UV damage that is actually degrading the surface. The money and the weekend are simply gone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Film-Forming Sealer for an Exposed Concrete Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic and epoxy coatings look excellent initially but wear in the traffic lanes, can be slippery when wet, and may cloud or peel if moisture is trapped beneath them. For an open driveway facing winter salt and freeze-thaw, a penetrating silane or siloxane is the more durable technical choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming One Product Covers a Mixed Driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An asphalt drive meeting a concrete apron needs two different products unless you have specifically sourced an acrylic labelled for both surfaces. Using one product across both to get a uniform appearance means half the driveway is protected by the wrong chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Concrete Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete needs to cure before it is sealed, typically around 28 days, and sealing early traps moisture in a slab that is still releasing it. The same principle applies to new asphalt, which needs its volatile oils to escape before it will accept a sealer bond. Both surfaces need patience before their first treatment.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between asphalt sealer and concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Asphalt sealer is an opaque black film applied on top of the surface to block UV light, which destroys the petroleum binder holding asphalt together. Concrete sealer is usually a clear penetrating liquid that soaks into the slab and repels water, because water and salt rather than sunlight are what damage concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use asphalt sealer on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt emulsion bonds to the bitumen in asphalt pavement and has nothing chemically compatible to grip on concrete. It peels and flakes, tracks onto shoes and tyres, traps moisture in the slab, and is difficult to remove. Use a sealer formulated for concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use concrete sealer on asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not effectively. Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers react with cementitious material, and asphalt has none, so they do not penetrate or protect. A clear film-forming acrylic will at least form a layer but lets UV through to the binder it should be shielding. Some acrylics are rated for both surfaces — check the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which lasts longer, asphalt sealer or concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete sealer, generally. A penetrating silane or siloxane sits inside the slab where traffic cannot wear it away and commonly lasts five to ten years. Asphalt emulsion sealer is a sacrificial surface film by design and needs renewing every two to three years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do concrete driveways need sealing at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They benefit from it, particularly in climates with freeze-thaw cycling and winter de-icing salt, which are the two things that spall concrete surfaces. A penetrating sealer keeps water out of the pore structure so it cannot freeze and lift the surface. It is less visually dramatic than sealing asphalt but the protective value is real.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sealer do I use for a driveway that is part asphalt and part concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the correct product on each section: an asphalt emulsion on the asphalt and a penetrating or acrylic concrete sealer on the concrete. Alternatively, look for an acrylic sealer explicitly labelled for both surfaces. Do not use a single asphalt product across both to get a uniform look.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put concrete sealer over asphalt sealer or vice versa?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Layering the two chemistries does not combine their benefits — the upper coating has nothing compatible to bond with and will peel. If a driveway has been sealed with the wrong product, that material needs removing before the correct sealer is applied.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

