        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            These are not two versions of the same product. Asphalt sealer is an opaque black sacrificial film that blocks the UV destroying your pavement’s petroleum binder. Concrete sealer is usually a clear liquid that soaks into the slab and stops water and salt from freezing inside it. Different enemy, different mechanism, different chemistry.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Match the product to the surface and neither substitution is tempting. Asphalt sealer on concrete peels, tracks and traps moisture. Concrete sealer on asphalt does essentially nothing, quietly, for the price of a weekend. The only legitimate crossover is an acrylic explicitly labelled for both surfaces, which is genuinely useful on a mixed driveway.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For an exposed concrete driveway facing winter salt, a penetrating silane or siloxane is the durable answer at five to ten years. For asphalt, a two-coat emulsion on a two to three year cycle is the maintenance model the material was designed around. Both are cheap relative to replacing the driveway, which is the only real alternative.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are genuinely unsure which surface you have — and on an old driveway that has been sealed repeatedly, that happens — check a broken edge or an unsealed margin for the colour underneath, and press a thumbnail into the surface on a hot afternoon. Asphalt gives slightly; concrete does not.
          </p>
        </div>

